use ../lib/CodingRule

from ./CertC import CertC

export coding-rule CertCErr02C:
    standard: @{CertC}
    ruleId: ERR02-C
    title: Avoid in-band error indicators
    statement:
        When designing a function, report errors separately from the result,
        for example by returning a status code and passing the result back
        through a pointer argument. Do not reserve one value of the result
        type to mean failure.
    rationale:
        A value that doubles as an error marker is easily mistaken for a
        valid result or forgotten by callers, as happens with EOF and with a
        negative size returned in a signed type. CERT rates likelihood
        unlikely, detectable no, repairable no, priority P1, level L3.
    exceptions:
        Null pointers may still signal failure because the language supports
        them. A function may also use an in-band indicator when the program
        is guaranteed not to keep processing after an error.
    severity: low
    noncompliant:
        ```c
        \\\
        /* Returns the port, or -1 on error. */
        int parse_port(const char *s);
        \\\
        ```
    compliant:
        ```c
        \\\
        /* Returns 0 on success and stores the port in *port. */
        int parse_port(const char *s, unsigned short *port);
        \\\
        ```
    languages: [c]
    tags: [recommendation, error-handling, api-design]
    source: https://cmu-sei.github.io/secure-coding-standards/sei-cert-c-coding-standard/recommendations/error-handling-err/err02-c
